---
// /repairers-and-recyclers — the passport from the side of the people who
// repair, remanufacture, repurpose and recycle products.
//
// What each reader may see is read from data/access.json (the battery
// passport's Art. 77(2) access rules, the same file /visibility uses), so the
// two pages cannot disagree. Checked 2026-09-26 against Regulation (EU)
// 2023/1542 Art. 77 (consolidated text 02023R1542-20250731) and the released
// node's credential behaviour: node-issued credentials last at most 90 days
// and cannot be withdrawn, and a credential is accepted on its own, without
// proof that the person presenting it is its holder.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import access from "../data/access.json";

const title = "For repairers and recyclers: reading a Digital Product Passport | Odal Node";
const description =
  "What a repairer, remanufacturer, second-life operator or recycler can read in a battery passport, how to get the credential that opens the restricted parts, and what happens to the passport when the battery changes hands or is recycled.";

const point = (id: string) => access.points.find((p) => p.id === id)!;
const open = point("1");
const withCredential = [point("2"), point("4")];

const mailto = `mailto:contact@odal-node.io?subject=${encodeURIComponent("Reading passports as a repairer or recycler")}`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Section h1 eyebrow="For repairers and recyclers" title="The parts of a passport made for your work." wide>
    <p>
      A battery passport has parts anyone can read and parts the law keeps for
      readers with a legitimate interest: repairers, remanufacturers,
      second-life operators and recyclers, for taking the battery apart safely
      and knowing what is inside it, and the buyer of an individual battery. On
      a passport served by Odal Node, those parts open with a credential.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page covers the battery passport, the first one the law requires,
      from 18 February 2027. For other product groups, no act sets these
      access rights yet.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="What you can read" title="Open to anyone, and open with a credential." tone="muted" wide>
    <div class="not-prose grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card m-0 p-5">
        <p class="m-0 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Without a credential</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-base font-semibold text-neutral-900">{open.title}</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-sm leading-body text-neutral-700">{open.note}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card m-0 border-primary-300 p-5">
        <p class="m-0 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary-700">With a credential</p>
        {withCredential.map((p) => (
          <>
            <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-base font-semibold text-neutral-900">{p.title}</p>
            <p class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-sm leading-body text-neutral-700">{p.note}</p>
          </>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6">
      Test reports proving compliance are for authorities only, and a
      battery's own history is for readers with a legitimate interest only:
      neither group sees everything. <a href="/visibility">Visibility</a> shows
      the split field by field, and the <a href="/example-passport">example
      passport</a> shows a complete one.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Getting a credential" title="Two ways to be let in." wide>
    <ol class="not-prose m-0 grid list-none gap-5 p-0 md:grid-cols-2">
      <li class="card m-0 p-5">
        <p class="m-0 text-base font-semibold text-neutral-900">From the company behind the product</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-sm leading-body text-neutral-700">
          The company that runs the passport can issue you a credential from its
          own node, naming your role: repairer, recycler, remanufacturer,
          preparer for re-use or distributor. It lasts up to 90 days, and you
          ask for a new one when it ends.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card m-0 p-5">
        <p class="m-0 text-base font-semibold text-neutral-900">From an issuer the company trusts</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-sm leading-body text-neutral-700">
          A company can also accept credentials from other issuers, such as a
          trade body, and chooses which ones. Where that issuer publishes a
          list of withdrawn credentials, the node checks it.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-6">
      There is no EU-wide issuer of these credentials yet. EU law lets the
      Commission set out how credentials are issued and checked, and the
      Batteries Regulation required it to specify, by 18 August 2026, who
      counts as having a legitimate interest and what they may do with the
      information. Until the software follows those acts, it applies the
      Regulation's own wording.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Keep it safe" title="Treat a credential like a password." tone="muted">
    <p>
      A credential works on its own: anyone holding a copy can use it until it
      expires or its issuer withdraws it. Keep it inside the business doing the
      work. A credential issued by a company's own node cannot be withdrawn,
      which is why it lasts 90 days at most.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="After the sale" title="When the battery changes hands, and when it ends." wide>
    <ul class="not-prose m-0 grid list-none gap-5 p-0 md:grid-cols-3">
      <li class="card m-0 p-5">
        <p class="m-0 text-base font-semibold text-neutral-900">Repurposed or remanufactured</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-sm leading-body text-neutral-700">
          Whoever puts a re-used, repurposed or remanufactured battery back on
          the market takes over the passport duties, and the battery gets a new
          passport linked to the original.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card m-0 p-5">
        <p class="m-0 text-base font-semibold text-neutral-900">Waste</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-sm leading-body text-neutral-700">
          Once a battery becomes waste, the duties pass to its producer, the
          producer's take-back scheme, or the waste operator chosen to handle
          it.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card m-0 p-5">
        <p class="m-0 text-base font-semibold text-neutral-900">Recycled</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-sm leading-body text-neutral-700">
          After the battery has been recycled, its passport ceases to exist.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-6">
      Odal Node records a handover inside the passport, signed by both sides,
      and exports the whole record as one signed file anyone can check
      without a network connection. <a href="/lifecycle">Lifecycle</a> follows
      a passport from start to end.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Check it is genuine" title="Verify a passport yourself." tone="muted">
    <p>
      Every passport served by Odal Node is signed with an identity on the
      issuing company's own web domain. Drop its proof file into{" "}
      <a href="/verify">Verify</a> to check the signatures and history in your
      browser, without Odal.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Talk to us" title="Reading passports in your workshop or plant." tone="tinted">
    <p>
      If you repair, remanufacture or recycle batteries and want to try
      reading passports with a credential, write to us and say what you handle
      and which systems you use.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <Button href={mailto}>Email contact@odal-node.io</Button>
      <Button href="/visibility" variant="secondary">See who reads what</Button>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
